{
  "_meta": {
    "source_key": "HSE-WIS30",
    "document": "HSE Woodworking Information Sheet No 30 (Revision 1), 'Toxic woods', first published 11/12",
    "read_from": "Internet Archive capture https://web.archive.org/web/20130213131629id_/http://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/wis30.pdf (same content digest as captures through 2019-11-13); HSE no longer hosts the document (404)",
    "accessed": "2026-11-07",
    "how_to_read": "Keys are the timber names exactly as WIS30 Table 2 prints them. WIS30 Table 2 gives TRADE NAMES ONLY - it gives no botanical names (the only botanical name in the whole sheet is Tectona grandis, in the precautions section, for teak). Effects are restated in our own words. 'categories' maps each effect to the species-schema hazard type enum (plus 'other' where nothing fits); the mapping is our interpretation, and WIS30 does not distinguish irritant from allergic for most entries - treat 'skin_irritant' as 'skin effect reported', not as a finding that it is non-allergic. 'asthma' is mapped to respiratory_sensitizer by convention; WIS30 Table 2 does not itself say whether the asthma is allergic.",
    "project_mapping_note": "project_species_file / project_botanical_name / project_name_verified come from this repo's data/species/*.json (assigned by the species agents, verified against WFO in their files where flagged). They are NOT stated by WIS30. Trade names like 'mahogany', 'ebony', 'rosewood', 'walnut (not African)', 'padauk' cover several species and WIS30 does not say which; the project file may be narrower than WIS30 intended.",
    "flags": "plywood = WIS30 marks the timber '#' (used for plywood); softwood = WIS30 marks it '$'.",
    "general_text_facts": [
      "WIS30 general text names western red cedar and iroko as examples where rare extrinsic allergic alveolitis has been seen.",
      "WIS30 names western red cedar as an example of wood dust causing allergic asthma.",
      "WIS30 names South African boxwood as an example of rare whole-body effects from inhaled dust (not in Table 2).",
      "WIS30 names greenheart and mansonia as examples of slow-healing, often septic splinter wounds.",
      "WIS30 notes nasal cancer as a very rare effect associated with hardwood dust.",
      "WIS30 states WELs of 5 mg/m3 for both hardwood and softwood dust - SUPERSEDED (EH40 2020: hardwood 3, softwood 5). Do not cite WIS30 for limits.",
      "WIS30 states inclusion in its table does not mean use will necessarily cause ill health, and woods not listed may also be toxic."
    ]
  },
